WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_saf_111'), 'The ticket that opened the incident, and the trace of one checkout today:

TICKET ORD-412: OrderService.ts is 900 lines. It validates the cart, charges the card, writes the order, sends the receipt and updates progress. Every change to it conflicts with someone else''s. Proposal: split it into orders-service, payments-service and notifications-service, each with its own repository and deploy pipeline.

Trace POST /orders (one process, codedev-api):
  OrderService.placeOrder        142 ms
    PaymentGateway.charge         98 ms  (Stripe test API)
    db.transaction                 21 ms  (INSERT orders, INSERT enrollments)
    EmailSender.sendReceipt       11 ms

Statement: "Deploying orders, payments and notifications as three separate services is the smallest change that fixes the problem described in the ticket."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. The ticket describes a code problem: one class does five jobs, so every change touches the same file. That is solved by modules with clear boundaries inside the same deployable (an orders module that calls a payments module through a small public function), which is a refactor of one repository and changes nothing at run time. Three services would also need a network call where the trace shows an in-process call, a way to handle payments-service being down, three pipelines, and a replacement for the single db.transaction that writes the order and the enrollment together. The misconception is that a separate service is the only way to get a boundary: a module is a boundary in the code, a service is a boundary in deployment, and the ticket only needs the first.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_saf_111') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
