    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_saf_211'), '// orders/OrderService.ts imports only ./ports (PaymentGateway, EmailSender, OrderRepository) and ./types

class FakePayments implements PaymentGateway {
  calls: string[] = [];
  constructor(private readonly result: ChargeResult) {}
  charge(orderId: string, learnerId: string, amountVnd: number): ChargeResult {
    this.calls.push(`${orderId} ${amountVnd}`);
    return this.result;
  }
}

test("a declined card saves a failed order and sends no receipt", () => {
  const emails = new FakeEmails();
  const orders = new InMemoryOrders();
  const service = new OrderService({
    payments: new FakePayments({ status: "declined", reason: "card_declined" }),
    emails,
    orders,
  });
  const result = service.placeOrder(an, authCourse);
  expect(result).toEqual({ ok: false, error: "payment_declined", reason: "card_declined" });
  expect(orders.saved.map((o) => o.status)).toEqual(["payment_failed"]);
  expect(emails.sent).toHaveLength(0);
});

The CI job that runs this file has no network access.

Statement: "This test can pass in that CI job, and it checks the declined-card path without needing a special card number from the payment provider''s sandbox."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. OrderService receives its payment gateway, mailer and repository through the constructor and only knows the port interfaces, so the test decides what the gateway answers: here a decline, with no HTTP call at all. Nothing in the code under test opens a connection, so a CI job without network can run it, and the declined path is as easy to reach as the happy path. Before the refactor, placeOrder created HttpPaymentClient itself, so every test talked to the sandbox and a decline needed the provider''s magic card numbers. The misconception is that you need the real provider (or its sandbox) to test payment logic: you need it to test the adapter, not the rules in OrderService.', 1, 5
