    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_saf_211'), '// orders/ports.ts - written during the refactor by copying the SDK''s method
import type { ChargeRequest, VendorCharge } from "@acme-pay/sdk";

export interface PaymentGateway {
  createCharge(body: ChargeRequest): VendorCharge; // { id, outcome: "approved" | "card_declined" | "insufficient_funds" }
}

// orders/OrderService.ts
const charge = this.deps.payments.createCharge({
  customer: learner.id, amount: course.priceVnd, currency: "VND", idempotencyKey: orderId,
});
if (charge.outcome !== "approved") { /* save payment_failed, return payment_declined */ }

The team plans to add a second payment provider whose API answers { state: "SUCCESS" | "REJECTED", transactionRef }.

Statement: "Because OrderService now depends on an interface instead of the HttpPaymentClient class, adding the second provider only needs a new adapter; OrderService itself will not change."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. The interface is a copy of one vendor''s API: its request and result types come from @acme-pay/sdk, and OrderService branches on that vendor''s outcome strings. A provider that answers state and transactionRef cannot implement createCharge(ChargeRequest): VendorCharge without pretending to be Acme, so either the adapter fakes Acme''s types or OrderService and its tests change. A port is useful when it is written in the application''s words (charge(orderId, learnerId, amountVnd) returning succeeded/declined) and owned by the code that uses it; translating to a vendor is the adapter''s job. The misconception is that any interface decouples you; an interface shaped like the vendor keeps the coupling and only adds a layer.', 2, 5
