VALUES ('chal_coding_saf_212', (SELECT id FROM catalog.course WHERE public_id = 'software-architecture-fundamentals'), (SELECT id FROM course.module WHERE course_id = (SELECT id FROM catalog.course WHERE public_id = 'software-architecture-fundamentals') AND order_index = 2 AND is_deleted = false LIMIT 1), (SELECT id FROM course.lesson WHERE content_source = 'lesson-saf-2-1' AND is_deleted = false LIMIT 1), 'order-service-ports', 'Put payment and email behind ports in OrderService', 'OrderService.placeOrder sells a premium CodeDev course. Today it creates HttpPaymentClient and SmtpEmailClient inside placeOrder, so it only works with the network and no test can make a card decline or the mail server fail. Refactor it so that the class is built as new OrderService({ payments, emails, orders }), where payments is a PaymentGateway, emails an EmailSender and orders an OrderRepository (the three port interfaces are given in the starter and must not change), and placeOrder uses only these ports. Keep the behaviour exactly as specified above placeOrder: an invalid price (not a positive whole number) gives invalid_price and an existing paid order gives already_owned, both without charging; otherwise take an id from orders.nextId() and call payments.charge(orderId, learnerId, priceVnd); if charge throws, return payment_unavailable and save nothing; a declined charge saves the order as payment_failed with chargeId null, sends no email and returns payment_declined with the reason; a successful charge saves the order as paid with the charge id, then calls emails.sendReceipt(email, orderId, courseTitle, priceVnd) and returns receiptSent true, or receiptSent false when sending throws. The test runner reads { paidBefore?, payments?, emails?, checkouts } from stdin, builds OrderService with recording fakes, places each checkout and prints the result with every port call, then a summary line (summary only for more than 10 checkouts). Wiring the real clients through adapters is not graded.', 'open', 'easy'::difficulty_question, 'mini_coding', 20, true, false)
