Evidence D - RFC-031 "Split the platform into microservices":
Services (7): catalog, orders, payments, notifications, progress, users, leaderboard
Each service: own repository, own pipeline, own database; HTTP calls and events between them
Expected benefits: "deploys get fast because each service is small",
                   "catalog can scale on its own",
                   "a bug in one service cannot break the others"

Statement: "The Monday evening spike shows that the catalog needs its own service so that it can scale independently."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. At the peak the app uses 71% CPU on two instances with 2 vCPU each, and Postgres 38%: nothing is saturated. A third instance of the whole app during the Monday window, or caching catalog pages that are read about 50 times for every write, covers the spike for far less than a new service with its own pipeline, database and on-call. Independent scaling pays when one part needs very different resources from the rest, or scaling the whole app becomes expensive; C shows neither. The misconception is "there is a spike, so split the part with the spike" without a number that says the whole app cannot absorb it.', 3, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_saf_311') AND order_index = 3)
    RETURNING id
)
