Statement: "For this team, keeping one deployable app with enforced module boundaries, plus written triggers that would reopen the decision, fits the evidence better than the seven-service plan."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. A shows one team of six owning every module, so separate services would not give separate teams independent deploys; B points to a serial test step and a shared table, both fixable directly; C shows spare capacity; E shows that the first extraction added a failure across three modules. A modular monolith makes the boundaries real now (public API per module, one owner per table, one-way dependencies) and keeps the option to extract a module later. The triggers - a second team, a module blocked from deploying, a module that needs very different scaling, repeated failures crossing modules - say when to look again. The misconception is that "monolith" means "no structure", or that the decision is made once and forever.', 5, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_saf_311') AND order_index = 5)
    RETURNING id
)
