WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_saf_331'), 'Signals sheet, monthly review (January 2027), one team of 6:
Module        blocked deploys/mo  peak load vs the rest                 teams  incidents crossing modules (quarter)
catalog       0                   about the same                        1      0
orders        0                   about the same                        1      0
leaderboard   0                   Sun 20:00-20:40: CPU 100%, RSS 3.4 GB  1      2 (instance restarted out of memory,
                                  (recompute job, in the app process)           checkout returned 502 for 6 and 9 min)
progress      0                   about the same                        1      0

APM, POST /orders p95: 310 ms normally, 2.4 s on Sundays 20:00-20:40.

Statement: "These numbers are a measured reason to run the leaderboard recompute in a separate process from the one that serves checkout."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. Two of the four signals are measured and point the same way: the recompute needs very different resources from the rest (100% CPU and 3.4 GB for 40 minutes a week), and twice in a quarter its failure took checkout down with it (out-of-memory restarts, 502s). The other signals stay at zero (one team, no blocked deploys), so the smallest step that answers them is to run the job in its own worker process, from the same codebase and the same deploy, on its own instance. A separate service with its own database is not required by this evidence. The misconception is that a split must be all or nothing: a separate process isolates resources and failures long before you need a separate repository.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_saf_331') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
