WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_saf_331'), 'Extraction plan for payments (draft):
Week 1, Monday:  create payments-service (own repository, own database, HTTP API)
Week 1, Friday:  deploy it; switch 100% of PaymentGateway.charge calls to it in the same release;
                 delete src/modules/payments the same day "to avoid two code paths";
                 copy the payments table with a one-off script during the release
Rollback plan:   restore from the backup taken before the release

Statement: "This plan follows the strangler approach to extracting a module."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. The strangler approach moves traffic gradually while the old path keeps working: put the module behind its port, add an adapter that calls the new service, route a small share of calls to it (or run it in shadow and compare results), raise the share as the numbers stay clean, and delete the old code only when the new path has carried all traffic for a while. This plan is a big-bang cutover: 100% of charges on day one, the old code deleted the same day, the data copied once, and a rollback that means restoring a backup and losing every payment made since. The misconception is that keeping two code paths for a while is waste; it is what lets you switch back in seconds instead of hours.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_saf_331') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
