WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_saf_341'), 'docs/adr/0007-keep-a-modular-monolith.md (accepted 2026-10-16)
Context: one team of 6 owns all modules. Deploys take a median of 20 min, 12.5 min of it a
serial test step. Both failed deploys in September came from migration 0412 on the orders
table, which three modules read directly. Catalog peak: 71% app CPU on 2 instances.
INC-77: the first extraction (notifications) sent 37 receipts for declined cards.
Options considered: (1) RFC-031, seven services with their own databases;
(2) one deployable app with enforced module boundaries; (3) no change.
Decision: option 2.

Statement: "A developer who joins in a year, after the team has changed, can tell from this ADR why the seven-service plan was not chosen."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. The context records the facts the decision rested on, with numbers (team size, where the 20 minutes go, the cause of both failed deploys, the CPU peak, the INC-77 impact), and the options section shows that RFC-031 was considered rather than ignored. A reader can check each fact against today and see whether the reasons still hold, without asking anyone who was in the meeting. The misconception is that an ADR only needs the decision; without context and options the next reader can only guess, and usually reopens the debate from scratch.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_saf_341') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
