    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_saf_341'), 'First draft of docs/adr/0007-keep-a-modular-monolith.md (pull request #640), section "Re-evaluation triggers":
- Revisit this decision when the monolith becomes too big or too slow.

Statement: "Anyone on the team can check this trigger at the monthly review and agree whether it has fired."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. "Too big" and "too slow" have no number and no place to read them, so two people looking at the same dashboard can disagree, and the trigger fires whenever someone feels like reopening the debate. A checkable trigger names a measurement, a threshold and a source, for example: "a second team owns a module (CODEOWNERS)", "a module is blocked by other modules'' changes more than twice a month (CI dashboard)", "a failure in one module takes checkout down twice in a quarter (incident log)". The misconception is that writing a trigger at all is enough; a trigger without a number is an opinion.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_saf_341') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
